
part 2: Express.js - Quick Revision Notes
What is Express.js?
- Minimal, fast, and flexible Node.js web framework.
- Simplifies routing, handling requests/responses, and middleware integration.
Basic Setup:
npm init -y
npm install express
const express = require('express');
const app = express();
app.listen(3000, () => {
 console.log('Server running...');
});
Routing:
app.get('/', (req, res) => res.send("Home"));
app.post('/user', (req, res) => { ... });
app.put('/user/:id', (req, res) => { ... });
app.delete('/user/:id', (req, res) => { ... });
- req.params -> Route parameters (e.g., /user/:id)
- req.query -> Query parameters (e.g., /search?q=abc)
Middleware:
- Functions that run between request and response.
app.use(express.json()); // Parse JSON body
app.use((req, res, next) => {
 console.log(`${req.method} ${req.url}`);
 next();
});
CRUD API Example:
let notes = [];
app.post('/notes', (req, res) => {
 const note = req.body;
 notes.push(note);
 res.status(201).json(note);
});
app.get('/notes', (req, res) => res.json(notes));
Status Codes:
- 200 OK: Success
- 201 Created: Resource created
- 400 Bad Request: Client error
- 404 Not Found: Resource not found
- 500 Internal Server Error: Server crash
Best Practices:
- Use .env + dotenv for environment variables
- Organize code: routes, controllers, middleware folders
- Always validate request body
